WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.802
-I want to ask about the fact
that you have been covering

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:07.173
Black Lives Matter
as a movement since 2012.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:11.677
Can you speak to, you know, how
you feel the movement has grown

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:13.947
and where you are currently at

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:16.349
in regards to optimism
or pessimism?

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.417
-Yeah, I mean, you know, when
I was covering even in 2012,

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:20.553
I was aware that,
you know, Black Lives Matter,

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.855
it started, you know,
during enslavement, right?

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:24.424
I mean, Black Lives Matter

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.426
is an age-old movement
that just changes names.

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.761
It's been
the Civil Rights Movement.

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.429
You know, it's been run by SNCC.

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:32.231
It's been run by the Southern
Christian Leadership Conference.

00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:33.933
It's been John Lewis' movement.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:35.935
You know,
it's been Dr. King's movement.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.437
And you know, in 2012,
it really launched

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:40.172
just as the term
"Black Lives Matter."

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:43.442
You know, three Black queer
women launched this hashtag.

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:46.645
And it was around
Trayvon Martin, this kid

00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:48.781
who's walking down the street
minding his own business.

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:50.450
He gets killed by somebody

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.318
who had an aspiration
of being a police officer.

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:54.453
So it had this sort of --
the flavor

00:00:54.453 --> 00:00:56.689
of police violence
against Black bodies.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:59.192
And so it launched around
this idea of saying, you know,

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.161
you need to think of these
young lives as being important,

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:05.298
and why we don't do that
in America is a big problem.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.733
And so Black Lives Matter
launched.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:10.603
And the thing that was so
different about then and now

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.006
is that at the time
you had a president

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:14.773
who was incredibly
empathetic to it.

00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:16.876
He said, "If I had a son,
he'd have looked like Trayvon

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.310
or I could have been Trayvon."

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.213
You had that empathy
in the White House,

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.981
that desire
to embrace the movement,

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.916
bring them into the White House,
talk to them.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:26.652
You know, Eric Holder was trying
to figure out, how could we work

00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:29.322
with Black Lives Matter
to make the country better?

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.123
But the public was against them.

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:33.793
You know, the polling was
terrible for Black Lives Matter.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.896
You know, white America
really rejected this movement

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.632
even when it was
Eric Garner and Michael Brown

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.067
and all of these people.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.401
You know, Freddie Gray.

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:44.036
I covered the Freddie Gray
movement in the streets.

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:45.938
You're talking about a guy
who was thrown limp

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:47.473
into the back of a police car,

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:49.542
and there was still
very little empathy.

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.412
Even Tamir Rice,
who was a baby, 12 years old.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:55.815
Nothing seemed
to move the public at large

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:58.484
other than Black folk
and Brown folk to be for it.

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.587
Now you have
the flip side of that.

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:02.154
It's completely upside down.

00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:04.723
You have a president
who's completely unsympathetic

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:06.292
and hostile
to Black Lives Matter,

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:09.228
hostile to the idea
of people kneeling, you know,

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.864
saying that we want fairness
and justice from police.

00:02:11.864 --> 00:02:13.632
But the public is now on

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:16.101
the Black Lives Matter
movement's side.

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:18.805
And that is a huge change,
and it's an important change.

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:20.807
You've now got
white folks out there

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:22.542
marching and
getting tear gassed.

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:24.644
Moms in Portland
getting tear gassed

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.378
for Black Lives Matter.

00:02:26.378 --> 00:02:28.714
Before, it was Heather Heyer
putting her life on the line

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:30.516
and losing her life
for Black Lives Matter.

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:32.084
Now it's a bunch of moms,

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:33.853
and I think
it's an important difference

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.388
because it means it's broader.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.290
This is not a niche movement.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.359
It's not just
a Black people concern.

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:43.362
This is the public realizing if
they're coming for Black lives,

00:02:43.362 --> 00:02:45.264
they're eventually
coming for yours.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:47.700
They're eventually coming
for your security and peace.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.235
All of us have to be
in this together.

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.003
I think that is actually
an important change.

00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:52.271
And we're seeing legislation.

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:54.173
We're actually seeing it
become legislation.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:57.343
So that's important.
-It's obviously also important

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.078
to have voices like yours
talking about this.

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:03.148
You're the only Black woman
anchoring a news show right now,

00:03:03.148 --> 00:03:05.584
and we're really lucky
to have you doing that.

00:03:05.584 --> 00:03:07.753
I want to leave by asking,

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:11.891
are you preferring these hours
to your "AM Joy" hours

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:14.827
when you hosted a morning show
on MSNBC?

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:18.798
-Well, yes and no.
Okay, so, on the "no" part,

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:21.233
two hours is like --
Think about how much you pay

00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:23.502
for a 30-second
television commercial, right?

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:25.804
Two hours is like gold, right?
You have two whole hours.

00:03:25.804 --> 00:03:28.140
So it's harder
to shrink myself...

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.775
and all the questions I have.

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:31.410
I have like a hundred questions
for Joe Biden.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:32.745
I can only ask like 40 of them.
Right?

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.314
You have to shrink it down.

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:36.649
And also I love
having Mondays off.

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:39.718
People do not understand
Monday being Saturday

00:03:39.718 --> 00:03:41.821
and Tuesday being Sunday
is everything.

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:43.389
You can get all your stuff done.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.191
Nobody's in the stores.
It's, like, perfect.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.160
So I'm going to miss
having Mondays and Tuesdays

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.395
as my weekend,
but I love these hours

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.430
in the sense
that it's immediate.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:55.601
Like, I'm not analyzing the week
from the back end.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:59.271
We're literally now front line,
every day, in the mix.

00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:01.607
For a news junkie,
there's nothing better

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:04.376
than being on the air
every night in prime time.

00:04:04.376 --> 00:04:06.846
It's just --
It's a fabulous opportunity.

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:10.349
-Well, congratulations again,
and hopefully when this

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:12.084
all clears up, we'll see
each other in the building.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:13.453
All the best, Joy.

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:15.755
-I will wave through
the German tourists at you.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:17.724
[ Laughter ]
-All right. Sounds good.

